---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Lesson 4: Conditional Statements with Arduino">
    <main class="min-h-screen bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-indigo-100 py-12">
        <div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-4xl">
            <div class="text-center mb-8">
                <div class="flex items-center justify-center mb-4">
                    <div class="bg-blue-500 text-white rounded-full w-16 h-16 flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-2xl mr-4">4</div>
                    <div class="text-left">
                        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-800">Lesson 4: Conditional Statements with Arduino</h1>
                        <p class="text-gray-600">Teach your program how to make decisions with `if`, `else`, and comparisons</p>
                        <p class="text-sm text-blue-600 font-medium">32-Lesson Course • Semester 1</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 rounded-r-lg p-4 mb-8">
                <p class="text-blue-900 font-medium">You already know variables, calculations, and Serial output. Now it is time to make your programs choose between different actions. Conditional statements let Arduino decide what to do based on a value or situation.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-4">Learning Objectives</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-600 mb-2">By the end of this Lesson, you will:</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-blue-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Understand what `if` and `else` do in Arduino code</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-blue-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Use comparison operators like `==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, and `>=`</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-blue-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Write simple decision-making code with the Serial Monitor</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-blue-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Control an LED differently depending on a button state or a value</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-green-600 mb-2">Skills Developed:</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-green-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Tracing program flow step by step</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-green-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Testing different outcomes using values and inputs</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-green-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Reading conditions carefully</li>
                            <li class="flex items-start"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-green-500 rounded-full mt-2 mr-3 flex-shrink-0"></span>Preparing for loops, functions, and hardware control later</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 1: What Is a Conditional Statement?</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">A conditional statement tells the program to check whether something is true. If it is true, one block of code runs. If it is false, a different block can run instead.</p>
                <div class="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4">
                    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-blue-800 mb-2">Everyday idea</h3>
                    <p class="text-blue-900">If it is raining, bring an umbrella. Else, leave the umbrella at home. Arduino code works the same way: it checks a condition and then decides which action to take.</p>
                </div>
                <pre class="text-green-400 whitespace-pre-wrap bg-gray-900 p-4 rounded text-sm overflow-x-auto">{`if (condition) {
  // code to run if the condition is true
} else {
  // code to run if the condition is false
}`}</pre>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 2: Comparison Operators</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">A condition usually compares two values. These comparison operators help Arduino decide if something is true or false.</p>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Useful operators</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700 text-sm">
                            <li><code>==</code> means equal to</li>
                            <li><code>!=</code> means not equal to</li>
                            <li><code>&lt;</code> means less than</li>
                            <li><code>&gt;</code> means greater than</li>
                            <li><code>&lt;=</code> means less than or equal to</li>
                            <li><code>&gt;=</code> means greater than or equal to</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-yellow-800 mb-2">Important detail</h3>
                        <p class="text-yellow-700 text-sm"><code>=</code> assigns a value. <code>==</code> compares two values. Beginners often mix these up, so read conditions carefully.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 3: A First `if` Statement</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Start with a simple value in a variable. Then let the program decide what message to print.</p>
                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        📋 Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);

  int score = 85;

  if (score >= 70) {
    Serial.println("You passed.");
  }
}

void loop() {
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg mt-4">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-green-800 mb-2">What happens?</h3>
                    <ul class="space-y-2 text-green-700">
                        <li><strong>The program stores `85` in `score`</strong></li>
                        <li><strong>It checks `score >= 70`</strong></li>
                        <li><strong>That condition is true</strong>, so the message prints</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 4: Using `if` and `else`</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">An `if` statement can also include an `else` block. That gives the program a different action when the condition is false.</p>
                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        📋 Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);

  int temperature = 62;

  if (temperature > 75) {
    Serial.println("It is warm.");
  } else {
    Serial.println("It is not warm.");
  }
}

void loop() {
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <p class="text-gray-700">Only one of these blocks runs each time the program checks the condition.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 5: More Than Two Choices with `else if`</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Sometimes a program needs more than just yes or no. `else if` lets you check another condition if the first one is false.</p>
                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        📋 Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);

  int score = 92;

  if (score >= 90) {
    Serial.println("Grade: A");
  } else if (score >= 80) {
    Serial.println("Grade: B");
  } else if (score >= 70) {
    Serial.println("Grade: C");
  } else {
    Serial.println("Keep practicing.");
  }
}

void loop() {
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-gray-50 p-4 rounded-lg mt-4">
                    <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-2">Order matters</h3>
                    <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm">Arduino checks from top to bottom. As soon as one condition is true, it runs that block and skips the rest.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 6: Using Conditionals with Hardware</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Decision-making becomes even more useful when the program reacts to a real input. A button can tell the Arduino when to turn an LED on or off.</p>
                <div class="bg-blue-50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4 text-blue-900">
                    This example uses a button on pin `2` and an LED on pin `8`. The button uses `INPUT_PULLUP`, so pressed usually reads as `LOW`.
                </div>
                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        📋 Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`const int ledPin = 8;
const int buttonPin = 2;

void setup() {
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(buttonPin, INPUT_PULLUP);
}

void loop() {
  int buttonState = digitalRead(buttonPin);

  if (buttonState == LOW) {
    digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);
  } else {
    digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);
  }
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <p class="text-gray-700">This is a real Arduino decision: if the button is pressed, the LED turns on. Else, it stays off.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Section 7: Common Beginner Mistakes</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 1</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Using <code>=</code> instead of <code>==</code> in a condition.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 2</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Forgetting that `INPUT_PULLUP` usually reads `LOW` when pressed.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 3</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Expecting every `if` block to run even after a true condition was already found in an `else if` chain.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h3 class="font-semibold text-red-800 mb-2">Mistake 4</h3>
                        <p class="text-red-700 text-sm">Not checking the actual value in the Serial Monitor while debugging.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-6">Hands-On Activity: Pass, Retry, or Excellent</h2>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4">Write a program that stores a score in a variable and prints one of three messages:</p>
                <div class="bg-yellow-50 p-4 rounded-lg mb-4 text-yellow-900">
                    <ul class="space-y-2">
                        <li>If the score is `90` or higher, print `Excellent work!`</li>
                        <li>Else if the score is `70` or higher, print `You passed.`</li>
                        <li>Else, print `Try again.`</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="relative">
                    <button onclick="copyCode(this)" class="absolute top-2 right-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs px-2 py-1 rounded transition-colors duration-200">
                        📋 Copy
                    </button>
                    <pre style="background: #1a202c; color: #e2e8f0; padding: 1rem; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; margin: 1rem 0;"><code>{`void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);

  int score = 76;

  if (score >= 90) {
    Serial.println("Excellent work!");
  } else if (score >= 70) {
    Serial.println("You passed.");
  } else {
    Serial.println("Try again.");
  }
}

void loop() {
}`}</code></pre>
                </div>
                <p class="text-gray-700 mt-4">Extension: change the value of `score` and predict what will print before you run the program.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-white rounded-xl shadow-lg p-6 mb-8">
                <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 mb-4">Assessment and Homework</h2>
                <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mb-6">
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">Lesson 4 quiz topics</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li>What `if`, `else`, and `else if` do</li>
                            <li>Comparison operators</li>
                            <li>How program flow changes based on a condition</li>
                            <li>Simple button and LED decision-making</li>
                            <li>Common conditional mistakes</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div>
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 mb-3">Homework ideas</h3>
                        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-700">
                            <li>Write two small `if`/`else` programs using different numbers</li>
                            <li>Change conditions and predict what will happen before running the code</li>
                            <li>Practice reading button logic with `INPUT_PULLUP`</li>
                            <li>Be ready to learn counted repetition and loops in Lesson 5</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="bg-green-50 p-4 rounded-lg text-green-900">
                    Strong programmers trace the condition carefully: what value do I have, what comparison am I making, and which block will run?
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="flex justify-between items-center mt-8">
                <div>
                    <a href="/programming/lessons32/Lesson3" style="background-color: #6b7280; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #4b5563; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#4b5563'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#6b7280'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                        ← Previous: Lesson 3
                    </a>
                </div>
                <div class="text-center">
                    <a href="/programming/quizzes32/Lesson4" style="background-color: #7c3aed; color: white; padding: 10px 16px; border-radius: 6px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 500; border: 2px solid #6d28d9; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#6d28d9'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#7c3aed'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                        Take Quiz 📝
                    </a>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <a href="/programming/lessons32/Lesson5" style="background-color: #2563eb; color: white; padding: 12px 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; border: 2px solid #1d4ed8; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1); transition: all 0.2s; display: inline-block;" onmouseover="this.style.backgroundColor='#1d4ed8'; this.style.transform='translateY(-2px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 10px 15px -3px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'" onmouseout="this.style.backgroundColor='#2563eb'; this.style.transform='translateY(0px)'; this.style.boxShadow='0 4px 6px -1px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1)'">
                        Next: Lesson 5 →
                    </a>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </main>
</Layout>

<script>
declare global {
    interface Window {
        copyCode: (button: HTMLButtonElement) => void;
    }
}

const lesson4Window = window as Window & typeof globalThis;

lesson4Window.copyCode = function(button: HTMLButtonElement) {
    try {
        const parentElement = button.parentElement;
        if (!parentElement) {
            showCopyFeedback(button, false);
            return;
        }

        const codeElement = parentElement.querySelector('code');
        if (!codeElement) {
            showCopyFeedback(button, false);
            return;
        }

        const codeText = codeElement.textContent || codeElement.innerText;

        if (navigator.clipboard && window.isSecureContext) {
            navigator.clipboard.writeText(codeText).then(() => {
                showCopyFeedback(button, true);
            }).catch(() => {
                fallbackCopy(codeText, button);
            });
        } else {
            fallbackCopy(codeText, button);
        }
    } catch (err) {
        showCopyFeedback(button, false);
    }
};

function fallbackCopy(text: string, button: HTMLButtonElement) {
    try {
        const textarea = document.createElement('textarea');
        textarea.value = text;
        textarea.style.position = 'absolute';
        textarea.style.left = '-9999px';
        textarea.style.top = '0';
        textarea.setAttribute('readonly', '');
        document.body.appendChild(textarea);

        textarea.select();
        textarea.setSelectionRange(0, 99999);

        const successful = document.execCommand('copy');
        document.body.removeChild(textarea);

        showCopyFeedback(button, successful);
    } catch (err) {
        showCopyFeedback(button, false);
    }
}

function showCopyFeedback(button: HTMLButtonElement, success: boolean) {
    const originalText = button.textContent;
    const originalBg = button.style.backgroundColor;

    if (success) {
        button.textContent = '✅ Copied!';
        button.style.backgroundColor = '#10b981';
    } else {
        button.textContent = '❌ Failed';
        button.style.backgroundColor = '#dc2626';
    }

    setTimeout((): void => {
        button.textContent = originalText;
        button.style.backgroundColor = originalBg || '#2563eb';
    }, 2000);
}
</script>
